rlm-git-status [-a|--all] [-b|--base <ref>] [<directory>]

Status roll-up across every git repo in a directory. Written for the
multi-repo collection dirs created by rlm-pr-worktree's multi-repo mode
— one directory holding a worktree of every repo on a shared branch —
where the question is "what in this change set is still unfinished?"

Scans the immediate children of <directory> (default: the current
directory), treating each child that is its own git worktree root as a
member. When the target is itself a repo and has no repo children, it
reports on just that repo, so the function also works as a plain
status summary from inside a single worktree.

Two independent dimensions are reported per repo, because a repo can
be any combination of them:

  VS <base>   Commit divergence from the base branch, shown as +N
              (ahead), -N (behind), both, "=" when in sync, or "?"
              when the base ref does not exist in that repo.
  LOCAL       Uncommitted work, broken down as conflict / staged /
              unstaged / untracked counts, or "clean".

By default only repos in either state are listed; the rest collapse
onto one dim "unchanged:" line, since in a typical 7-repo collection
only one or two differ and listing all of them buries the ones that
matter. Repo names on that line are abbreviated by stripping a shared
boilerplate prefix (data-platform-) when one is long enough to be
worth removing.

The base ref defaults to origin/<default branch>, read from
origin/HEAD and falling back to origin/main. Nothing is ever fetched,
so divergence is measured against the remote-tracking refs as they
stand locally — run `git fetch` first if they may be stale.

Repos are probed in parallel, so a 7-repo collection costs roughly one
git round of wall-clock rather than seven.

Colors are emitted only when stdout is a terminal, so the table stays
pasteable when piped or redirected.

OPTIONS
  -a, --all          List every repo, including clean ones, instead of
                     collapsing them onto the "unchanged:" line.
  -b, --base <ref>   Compare against <ref> instead of
                     origin/<default branch>.
  -h, --help         Print a usage line.

EXAMPLES
  git-status ~/git/work/StorytelDataPlatform_20260730_DATA-2770-foo
  git-status                      # current directory
  git-status --all                # include clean repos
  git-status --base origin/develop

REQUIRES
  git
